<div class="article-body">
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>What Dead Hangs Are Worth Doing For</h2>
        <p>A dead hang loads the grip, shoulders, trunk and spine in one static hold. Large observational studies link grip strength with health. The other benefits below are mechanisms and practitioner reports. Each section states its evidence level.</p>

        <div class="visual-block">
          <div class="dg-wheel" role="group" aria-label="Dead hang benefits wheel showing 7 key benefits radiating from center"><svg class="dg-wheel-lines" viewBox="0 0 660 660" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><circle cx="330" cy="330" r="280" fill="none" stroke="#2a3a54" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 6" /><line x1="330" y1="258" x2="330" y2="115" stroke="#2a3a54" stroke-width="1.5" /><line x1="392" y1="272" x2="525" y2="155" stroke="#2a3a54" stroke-width="1.5" /><line x1="402" y1="330" x2="570" y2="330" stroke="#2a3a54" stroke-width="1.5" /><line x1="392" y1="388" x2="525" y2="505" stroke="#2a3a54" stroke-width="1.5" /><line x1="330" y1="402" x2="330" y2="545" stroke="#2a3a54" stroke-width="1.5" /><line x1="268" y1="388" x2="135" y2="505" stroke="#2a3a54" stroke-width="1.5" /><line x1="258" y1="330" x2="90" y2="330" stroke="#2a3a54" stroke-width="1.5" /></svg><p class="dg-hub">Dead Hang Benefits</p><ul class="dg-spokes"><li style="left:42.12%;top:5.76%"><svg class="hf-icon" viewBox="0 0 32 28" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 22C6 22 4 14 8 10C12 6 16 8 16 12C16 8 20 6 24 10C28 14 26 22 26 22"/><line class="hf-hl" x1="10" y1="22" x2="22" y2="22"/></svg><span>Grip Strength</span></li><li style="left:73.94%;top:13.33%"><svg class="hf-icon" viewBox="0 0 32 28" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><circle cx="16" cy="8" r="4.5"/><path class="hf-hl" d="M4 26C4 19 10 15 16 15C22 15 28 19 28 26"/><path d="M8 19L4 15M24 19L28 15"/></svg><span>Shoulder Health</span></li><li style="left:80.00%;top:42.12%"><svg class="hf-icon" viewBox="0 0 32 28" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><line class="hf-hl" x1="16" y1="4" x2="16" y2="24"/><polyline points="10,8 16,4 22,8"/><polyline points="10,20 16,24 22,20"/></svg><span>Spinal Decompression</span></li><li style="left:73.94%;top:70.91%"><svg class="hf-icon" viewBox="0 0 32 28" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><rect x="6" y="4" width="20" height="20" rx="3"/><path class="hf-hl" d="M6 14H26M16 4V24"/></svg><span>Core Stability</span></li><li style="left:42.12%;top:78.48%"><svg class="hf-icon" viewBox="0 0 32 28" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><circle cx="16" cy="14" r="7"/><path class="hf-hl" d="M4 12C4 3 28 3 28 12"/><path d="M12 17C12 22 20 22 20 17"/></svg><span>Mental Toughness</span></li><li style="left:10.30%;top:70.91%"><svg class="hf-icon" viewBox="0 0 32 28" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><circle cx="16" cy="5" r="3"/><line class="hf-hl" x1="16" y1="10" x2="16" y2="22"/><path d="M10 13L16 11L22 13"/><line x1="12" y1="24" x2="20" y2="24"/></svg><span>Posture Correction</span></li><li style="left:4.24%;top:42.12%"><svg class="hf-icon" viewBox="0 0 32 28" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path class="hf-hl" d="M16 3L18.5 10L26 10L20 14.5L22.3 22L16 17.5L9.7 22L12 14.5L6 10L13.5 10Z"/></svg><span>Longevity</span></li></ul></div>
          <p class="caption">Areas that dead hangs are used for. The sections below give the evidence level for each.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>1. Grip Strength and Forearm Endurance</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: training principle and not a dead hang trial. Hanging holds the forearm flexors under sustained isometric load. Grip endurance is the most direct thing a hang trains. Deadlifts, rows, pull-ups and carries all end when the hands open first. Grip endurance helps in all of these lifts.</p>
        <p>Dead hangs work three forearm muscles together: the flexor digitorum profundus, flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor pollicis longus. Fingertip push-ups and wrist curls work these muscles in different ways. Hangs are one option among several. The <a href="/grip-strength/">grip strength training guide</a> covers forearm programming in detail.</p>
        <p>A common starting point is 10 to 20 second holds. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly and roughly once a week. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

        <h2>2. The Spine</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: mechanism only. Hanging unloads the spine while you hang. No study has measured disc height or pressure during a bodyweight hang. A Cochrane review (Wegner et al. 2013) found traction gives little or no benefit for low back pain. Some people find short hangs ease stiffness.</p>
        <p>People are a little shorter in the evening than in the morning. No study shows that a hang reverses that. Short holds of 10 to 30 seconds are a common starting dose. No trial defines a minimum effective time. The <a href="/spine-decompression/">spine and dead hangs guide</a> sets out what is and is not known.</p>

        <h2>3. Shoulder Mobility</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: one surgeon's proposal that has not been tested. Orthopedic surgeon John M. Kirsch proposed overhead hanging for shoulder impingement in a self-published book (5th ed. 2019). No published randomized trial has tested it (Europe PMC search, October 2026).</p>
        <p>A hang puts the arms overhead. People commonly feel a stretch through the chest and the underside of the shoulder. No study cited here measures this.</p>
        <p>Not all shoulder pain responds to hanging. Rotator cuff tendinopathy can feel worse with hanging. Pain that improves with hanging does not identify its cause. See a clinician for a diagnosis. Start with short holds and stop if pain increases. The <a href="/shoulder-health/">shoulder health guide</a> has the details and the situations to avoid.</p>

        <h2>4. Upper Body Strength Foundation</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: general training principle. A dead hang is an isometric hold. It involves the lats, posterior deltoids, trapezius, rhomboids, forearm flexors and trunk muscles.</p>

