        <p>A hang trains grip and shoulder endurance directly. Pulling strength needs pulling practice. In a passive hang the lats are stretched. In an active hang they contract to hold the shoulder blades down. The <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">passive vs active hang guide</a> compares the two.</p>

        <h2>5. Posture and Desk Work</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: practitioner report. Desk work often leaves the chest tight and the shoulders rolled forward. A hang puts the chest and shoulders in an open overhead position. Many people find this a welcome change after hours of sitting. No study shows that hanging changes habitual posture.</p>
        <p>Some people hang for 15 to 30 seconds between work blocks. A <a href="/doorway-bars/">doorway pull-up bar</a> makes this easy at home.</p>

        <h2>6. Pull-Up Progression Starting Point</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: coaching convention. You cannot do a pull-up if you cannot hold the bar. The dead hang is therefore the first step in most pull-up progressions. A common rule of thumb is a 30 second hang with good form before starting negative pull-ups. Time to reach it varies widely with bodyweight and starting grip.</p>
        <p>Many people then move from a passive hang to an active hang with the shoulder blades pulled down. The active hang trains the bottom position of a pull-up. The <a href="/deadhang-progressions/">dead hang progression ladder</a> lays out the steps from assisted hangs to one-arm hangs.</p>

        <h2>7. Longevity and Grip Strength</h2>
        <p>Evidence level: observational association and not a hanging trial. The PURE cohort followed 139,691 adults aged 35 to 70 in 17 countries. Each 5 kg lower grip strength was associated with a 16% higher risk of death from any cause (Leong et al., Lancet 2015). Grip strength predicted all-cause mortality better than systolic blood pressure in that study. This grip finding is an association and not proof that hanging lengthens life.</p>
        <p>On The Peter Attia Drive (episode 329, December 2024) Peter Attia said a very strong 40-year-old man should manage at least two minutes and a very strong 40-year-old woman at least 90 seconds. He said to subtract about 10 seconds per decade. That is a podcaster's target for strong people, not measured data.</p>
        <p>A pooled analysis of 12 British studies with 49,964 participants (Dodds et al., PLOS ONE 2014) found a peak median grip of 51 kg in men aged 29 to 39 and 31 kg in women aged 26 to 42. Those are dynamometer readings in kilograms, not hang times.</p>
        <p>Grip strength is a marker in these studies. The studies cannot show that raising grip strength lengthens life. The <a href="/longevity-research/">longevity and grip strength research</a> page sets out the evidence in full.</p>

        <h2>Dead Hangs for Seniors, Office Workers and Athletes</h2>
        <p><strong>Seniors (age 65+):</strong> Dead hangs can suit older adults who have a sturdy bar and a safe step. Anyone with osteoporosis or shoulder problems should check with a clinician first. There is no validated hang time that predicts independence or health. Start with 5 to 10 second hangs and progress slowly.</p>
        <p><strong>Office and desk workers:</strong> Some desk workers use short hangs between work blocks to open the chest and shoulders. Hanging breaks up seated time. No study shows that hanging corrects posture.</p>
        <p><strong>Athletes in pulling sports (climbers, rowers):</strong> Dead hangs are a basic grip endurance exercise. Climbers often add grip variations such as one-arm holds, towel hangs and thick-bar hangs. A standard-bar hold does not match a thin edge. Some rowers use hangs as a grip and shoulder endurance exercise.</p>

        <h2>Contraindications by Spinal Condition: When to Avoid or Modify</h2>
        <p>Many healthy people can start with short supported hangs. People with certain spinal conditions need medical clearance or specific modifications. The severity of the condition matters more than the diagnosis.</p>
        <p><strong>Mild lower back stiffness:</strong> Some people with mild stiffness tolerate hanging well. Stop if symptoms worsen.</p>
        <p><strong>Chronic lower back pain (non-structural):</strong> Some people with non-structural back pain find hanging comfortable. Trials are lacking. Start with 5 to 10 second hangs on 3 non-consecutive days a week. If pain worsens then stop and see a physical therapist.</p>
        <p><strong>Mild disc bulge or protrusion (diagnosed):</strong> Get medical clearance before hanging. Follow your clinician's advice on how to start.</p>
        <p><strong>Moderate disc herniation or central spinal stenosis:</strong> Medical clearance is mandatory. Some people with these conditions tolerate hanging and others have more pain. A physician or physical therapist must assess your specific anatomy first.</p>
        <p><strong>Severe or acute disc herniation:</strong> Avoid dead hangs until symptoms settle and a spine specialist clears you. Hanging with an acutely herniated disc may worsen symptoms.</p>
        <p><strong>Recent spinal fusion or other spinal surgery:</strong> Follow your surgeon's timeline for returning to hanging.</p>
        <p>If you are in doubt then see a physical therapist or physician who can assess your condition and clear you for hanging.</p>

        <h2>Home Progress Tracking Without Equipment</h2>
        <p>Home tracking needs only a timer and a notebook. Record the dead hang duration in seconds and the sets completed. Also record the total hang time per session. Functional checks such as time to open a stubborn jar lid or a weekly 0 to 10 rating for lower back stiffness and shoulder comfort add context. Review progress monthly since week-to-week variation is normal.</p>

        <h2>How to Get Started</h2>
        <p>You need a bar that supports your bodyweight and enough overhead clearance to hang with arms fully extended. Hold lengths and session counts below are common training practice. They are not a tested prescription.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Quick-Start Protocol</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Grab the bar with an overhand grip at shoulder width.</li>
            <li>Step off the platform and let your bodyweight hang fully.</li>
            <li>Relax your shoulders and breathe through your nose.</li>
            <li>Hold for 10 to 30 seconds. Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out and step down under control.</li>
            <li>Rest 60 to 90 seconds between sets.</li>
            <li>Perform 3 sets in each of 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p>The <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">step-by-step dead hang form guide</a> covers grip placement, shoulder position and breathing. Time to a longer hold varies widely between people. Measure against your own earlier holds.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How long should I dead hang to get benefits?</h3>
          <p>Many people start with hangs of 10 to 30 seconds. No trial defines a minimum effective time for any benefit. Common training practice is 3 sets in each of 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days. This is not a tested prescription.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Are dead hangs good for your back?</h3>
          <p>Hanging unloads the spine while you hang. No study has measured disc height or pressure during a bodyweight hang. A Cochrane review (Wegner 2013) found traction gives little or no benefit for low back pain. Some people find short hangs ease stiffness. Avoid dead hangs with an acute disc herniation. Ask a clinician first if you have a diagnosed back condition.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Can dead hangs fix bad posture?</h3>
          <p>No study shows that dead hangs change habitual posture. A hang puts the chest and shoulders in an open overhead position. Many people find this welcome after desk work. Many people pair hangs with scapular retraction exercises if posture is a goal.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Do dead hangs build muscle?</h3>
          <p>Dead hangs mainly train grip and shoulder endurance. A static hold is not a typical muscle-building stimulus. Many people add pulling and rowing exercises for muscle growth. A hang does build the grip and shoulder endurance those exercises rely on.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Are dead hangs safe for shoulders?</h3>
          <p>Hanging suits some shoulders and aggravates others. Orthopedic surgeon John M. Kirsch argues that hanging eases impingement. No trial has tested this. If you have shoulder pain, see a clinician, start with 5 to 10 second holds, and stop if you feel sharp or stabbing pain.</p>
        </div>

